(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-cms-exam-4-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 4 Master Exam — Form D","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 57-year-old man had a drug-eluting stent placed two weeks ago for an acute coronary syndrome. He tells you the clopidogrel is expensive and he plans to stop it because his chest pain is gone. What should he be told?","choices":["Aspirin alone now protects the stent","Missed doses matter only if pain returns","Stopping now risks a clot in the stent","It can stop once the chest pain is gone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Stopping dual antiplatelet therapy (a P2Y12 receptor antagonist such as clopidogrel plus aspirin) in the first 6 weeks after a stent is the strongest predictor of stent thrombosis, a sudden clot that occludes the stent and can cause a large, often fatal infarction. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which two lipoproteins are the main transporters of triglycerides?","choices":["High-density lipoprotein and chylomicrons","Low-density lipoprotein and lipoprotein(a)","Chylomicrons and very-low-density lipoprotein","Low- and high-density lipoprotein"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Triglyceride transport belongs to chylomicrons (dietary fat) and very-low-density lipoprotein (made by the liver); cholesterol transport belongs to the low- and high-density classes. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman has had left-sided heart failure with reduced ejection fraction for five years. Over two months she has developed leg edema, jugular venous distension (distended neck veins), an enlarged liver, nausea and poor appetite. Which is the most likely explanation for her new findings?","choices":["High-output failure from increased demand","Worsening systolic failure of the left only","Primary liver disease with ascites","Right-sided failure from her left-sided failure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chronic left-sided failure is the most common cause of right-sided failure. Systemic overload gives edema and distended neck veins; portal overload gives nausea, poor appetite and hepatomegaly. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man with Marfan syndrome has a high-pitched blowing diastolic murmur at the third intercostal space at the left upper sternal border that radiates to the apex. He has bounding pulses and a wide pulse pressure. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Aortic regurgitation","Mitral regurgitation","Pulmonic regurgitation","Mitral stenosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Blowing means regurgitation and diastolic narrows it to the aortic or pulmonic valve; the third intercostal space with radiation to the apex, bounding pulses and a wide pulse pressure mark aortic regurgitation. Marfan syndrome is a root-disease cause. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 27, 28"},{"q":"Which drug class is recommended to prevent heart failure in patients with diabetes who have no structural heart disease?","choices":["Beta blocker started at diagnosis","Hydralazine with isosorbide dinitrate","Angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor","Sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Prevention in stage A, risk factors with a normal heart, carries one strong drug recommendation: a sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor for patients with diabetes, ahead of any beta blocker, which is a stage B therapy. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man on a newly increased diuretic keeps standing up quickly despite advice, and his pressure falls 30 mmHg on standing. If this continues, what is he most at risk of?","choices":["Diabetic autonomic neuropathy","Syncope from cerebral hypoperfusion","A seizure disorder","Supine hypertension overnight"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Syncope is the result of symptomatic hypotension: when the standing pressure falls far enough, brain perfusion drops and consciousness is briefly lost. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with reduced ejection fraction (fraction 30%) on sacubitril-valsartan, a beta blocker, spironolactone and empagliflozin develops atrial fibrillation, and rate control is being planned. Which medication should be avoided?","choices":["Sacubitril-valsartan","Spironolactone","Empagliflozin","Diltiazem"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Diltiazem is a calcium channel blocker that is avoided in heart failure with reduced ejection fraction. His existing beta blocker belongs to core therapy. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 73-year-old woman with chronic heart failure has a B-type natriuretic peptide that has risen by 100 since her last visit. What does this change imply?","choices":["About 90% higher relative risk of death","No change in her relative risk of death","About 5% higher relative risk of death","About 35% higher relative risk of death"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Each increase of 100 carries about a 35% increase in the relative risk of death, so a rising level is a warning sign even when symptoms seem stable. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Mitral regurgitation is backflow into which chamber?","choices":["The right ventricle, in diastole","The left atrium, in systole","The left ventricle, in diastole","The right atrium, in systole"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mitral regurgitation is incomplete closure of the mitral valve, so blood flows back into the left atrium while the ventricle contracts, leading to left atrial dilation with pressure and volume overload. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 44, 46"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman with long-standing hypertension has leg edema, crackles and breathlessness climbing stairs. Echocardiogram shows an ejection fraction of 62% and B-type natriuretic peptide is 640. Which therapy carries the strongest recommendation now?","choices":["An inotrope such as dobutamine","Hydralazine with isosorbide dinitrate","A diuretic as needed for congestion","A beta blocker titrated to heart rate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. With preserved ejection fraction and congestion, diuretics as needed carry the strong recommendation. A sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor is next at moderate strength; the neprilysin combination, antagonist and receptor blocker are weak. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man fainted at home and has recovered. His electrocardiogram and glucose are normal. Which tests look for an infectious or metabolic contributor?","choices":["A blood count and metabolic panel","A tilt-table test and a log","Orthostatic vitals only","An echocardiogram and stress test"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Unremarkable blood counts and a metabolic panel lower suspicion of an infectious or metabolic source, as glucose and the electrocardiogram address hypoglycemia and dysrhythmia. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with ischemic cardiomyopathy has an ejection fraction of 25%, is class II on optimal guideline-directed therapy, and has metastatic cancer with an expected survival of a few months. Which is most appropriate regarding an implantable defibrillator?","choices":["Implant it before discharge","Implant it once he is class IV","Implant it after a cardiac arrest","It is not indicated for him"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The device is indicated only with a life expectancy of at least one year, together with ejection fraction 35% or less, class II or III and optimal therapy. His expected survival of months fails that criterion. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which condition is associated with postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome?","choices":["Multiple system atrophy","Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","Long-standing diabetes","Parkinson disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ehlers-Danlos syndrome is the association given for the syndrome, which mostly affects women aged 15 to 50. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man has an average home blood pressure of 126/78. He has no diabetes, no kidney disease and no cardiovascular history. What should he be advised?","choices":["Lifestyle treatment","Start a thiazide-type diuretic now","No intervention is needed until he reaches stage 1","Recheck in five years with no advice in the interim"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An elevated reading that does not yet warrant medication still warrants an intervention, and lifestyle measures continue alongside drugs later rather than being replaced by them. It is recommended at every blood pressure category. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman with heart failure asks why she must take several medicines when she already feels better. Which explanation matches the goals of treatment?","choices":["They are meant to cure her cardiomyopathy","They are meant to make her ejection fraction normal","They are only needed while she has symptoms","They also extend life and slow progression"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The goals are to decrease symptoms, increase survival and limit disease progression. Feeling better meets only the first of the three, so the regimen continues. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 49-year-old newly diagnosed hypertensive patient has fundoscopy showing generalized arteriolar narrowing and arteriovenous nicking, with no hemorrhages or exudates. What does this tell you?","choices":["These findings carry no prognostic weight","They reflect current and previous pressure","These findings indicate a hypertensive emergency","These changes reflect current blood pressure only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Generalized narrowing and nicking record the accumulated burden, whereas focal narrowing, hemorrhages and exudates relate to CURRENT pressure only. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with a holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border that increases with inspiration is examined further. Which additional finding is most expected?","choices":["A collapsing radial pulse","Head bobbing with each beat","Delayed, weak carotid pulse","Elevated jugular pulsations"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tricuspid regurgitation raises the jugular venous pressure with visible neck pulsations, and there may be a venous thrill (a palpable vibration) over the right jugular vein. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man has a brief early diastolic murmur at the second left intercostal space from pulmonic regurgitation. Which maneuver makes the murmur louder?","choices":["Squatting","Standing","Sitting upright","Expiration"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Anything that increases venous return increases the pulmonic regurgitation murmur -- inspiration, squatting and lying supine. Expiration and standing decrease it. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 63, 9"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman has two days of worsening breathlessness and ankle edema. Her N-terminal pro-B-type natriuretic peptide is 1,150. What does this level suggest for her age?","choices":["Heart failure is unlikely at this level","The level is normal for her age","Acute heart failure is ruled out","Acute heart failure is likely"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. At ages 50 to 75, an N-terminal level above 900 makes acute heart failure likely; the cut-off is above 450 under age 50 and above 1,800 beyond 75. Age changes how the same number is read. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 59-year-old hypertensive man is breathless with bibasal crackles and raised jugular venous pressure. Is chest radiography appropriate?","choices":["Yes, as a routine part of every hypertensive workup","No, because chest radiography is never indicated in hypertension","No, because a normal film would exclude dissection","Yes, because findings suggest pulmonary edema"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Radiography is appropriate when dyspnea or findings suggest pulmonary edema, when cardiomegaly is suspected, or when a widened mediastinum is a concern. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with low high-density lipoprotein has improved his lifestyle, and his clinician is weighing a drug chosen specifically to raise high-density lipoprotein. Which drug raises it most?","choices":["Niacin","Ezetimibe","Colesevelam","Pravastatin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Niacin raises high-density lipoprotein 15% to 35%, better than other medications, compared with fibrates 10% to 30%, statins 5% to 22% and ezetimibe 8%. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man is newly found to have heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia with low-density lipoprotein of 250 mg/dL. He has read about a new injectable and wants it instead of any pill. Which option is inappropriate as initial therapy?","choices":["A high-intensity statin","A low saturated-fat, low-cholesterol diet","Addressing smoking and blood pressure","Evolocumab before trying a statin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The steps must be followed: high-intensity statins first, then ezetimibe (niacin, nicotinic acid, was once added but is no longer recommended), and the injectable only for patients refractory to drug therapy. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man who drinks heavily is admitted for an unrelated procedure. On the second day his blood pressure rises sharply. What explains this?","choices":["Moderate alcohol should be resumed for cardiovascular benefit","Alcohol lowers pressure, so stopping returns him to baseline","Alcohol withdrawal can produce acute severe hypertension","Only chronic excess raises pressure; withdrawal has no effect"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the relationship runs both ways: excess alcohol raises blood pressure chronically, and withdrawal from it can raise it acutely. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman develops a persistent dry cough six weeks after starting lisinopril. Her blood pressure is at goal. What is the appropriate response?","choices":["Stop all antihypertensive therapy","Switch to an angiotensin receptor blocker","Add an antitussive and continue the lisinopril","Add losartan to the lisinopril"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The cough is bradykinin-mediated and occurs in up to about one in five. Angiotensin receptor blockers do not raise bradykinin, so the effect is lost while the therapeutic action is kept. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman has a waist circumference of 38 inches, triglyceride of 190 mg/dL, high-density lipoprotein of 45 mg/dL, blood pressure of 134/88 and fasting glucose of 92 mg/dL. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Familial dysbetalipoproteinemia","Severe hypertriglyceridemia","Mixed dyslipidemia","Metabolic syndrome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Any three of five criteria establish it; she meets four: waist above 35 inches, triglyceride above 150, high-density lipoprotein below 50 in a woman and blood pressure above 130/85. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Nitroglycerin is withheld in a right ventricular infarct because the injured right ventricle depends on what?","choices":["A slow heart rate","Adequate preload","Relief of spasm","Extra oxygen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The infarcted right ventricle is preload dependent: nitroglycerin decreases preload, which reduces the Frank-Starling effect on its myocardium, so it fails into right-sided heart failure, output drops and shock follows. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with 20 years of poorly controlled hypertension has ankle swelling and crackles at both lung bases. Blood pressure is 176/98. A fourth heart sound (a sound in late diastole just before the first heart sound) is heard. Echocardiogram shows thick left ventricular walls with an ejection fraction of 62%. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Systolic failure from dilated cardiomyopathy","Systolic failure from ischemic cardiomyopathy","Diastolic failure from hypertensive heart disease","High-output failure from thyroid excess"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The diastolic chain is complete: long-standing hypertension thickening the ventricle, a fourth heart sound from atrial contraction against a stiff ventricle, ankle swelling with lung congestion, and a preserved ejection fraction. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with stable exertional angina has a documented contraindication to beta blockers. Which medication class is used instead?","choices":["Calcium channel blocker","Thrombolytic agent","Nonselective beta blocker","Phosphodiesterase inhibitor"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In stable angina, a calcium channel blocker takes the place of the beta blocker when beta blockers are contraindicated, alongside lifestyle change, a statin, aspirin and sublingual nitroglycerin. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with a mechanical aortic valve, atrial fibrillation and a prior embolic stroke is on warfarin and is scheduled for a colectomy that requires stopping it. Which plan should be avoided?","choices":["Rechecking his ratio after surgery","Resuming a vitamin K antagonist","Bridging with heparin","Stopping warfarin with no bridge"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. His mechanical aortic valve comes with atrial fibrillation and a prior clot, which put it at higher clot risk, so warfarin is bridged with heparin until the international normalized ratio is therapeutic; leaving the valve uncovered is the thing to avoid. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 60-year-old hypertensive man has an electrocardiogram that does not meet voltage criteria for left ventricular hypertrophy, but he is breathless on exertion and has a significant murmur. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Proceed to echocardiography","Repeat the electrocardiogram in six months","Conclude that hypertensive heart disease is excluded","Order a chest radiograph to measure the cardiac size"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The voltage criteria are specific but NOT sensitive, so false negatives are common. Unexplained dyspnea and a significant murmur are both indications for echocardiography in their own right. A normal tracing does not exclude hypertrophy. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man had a myocardial infarction a year ago. He has no symptoms of heart failure and no diabetes, and his echocardiogram shows systolic dysfunction. Which regimen is recommended?","choices":["Dapagliflozin alone, since he has no symptoms","A converting enzyme inhibitor and a beta blocker","Hydralazine with isosorbide dinitrate","A loop diuretic started at once"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Asymptomatic structural disease is stage B, pre-heart failure, where an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor and a beta blocker are strong recommendations; a receptor blocker replaces the inhibitor if it is not tolerated. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with reduced ejection fraction (fraction 30%) has gained weight with leg edema and crackles. She takes a beta blocker, sacubitril-valsartan, spironolactone and dapagliflozin. Which drug relieves her fluid overload now?","choices":["Diltiazem to slow the heart rate","Hydralazine given on its own","A loop diuretic such as furosemide","Dobutamine given at home"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Diuretics are used as needed for congestion in every ejection fraction group; loops such as furosemide and bumetanide are the potassium-wasting diuretics that clear fluid. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man is diagnosed with a non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction. His oxygen saturation (SpO2) is 97% on room air and his heart rate is 88. Which is part of his initial treatment?","choices":["Supplemental oxygen","Chewable aspirin","A thrombolytic agent","A calcium channel blocker"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Unstable angina and non-ST-elevation infarction are a medical emergency treated alike: chewable aspirin, a beta blocker and intravenous nitrates if appropriate, and dual antiplatelet therapy with or without anticoagulation. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 49, 80"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has an echocardiogram for palpitations that incidentally shows mild aortic stenosis. He has no chest pain, breathlessness or fainting. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["No follow-up is needed","Cardiology consultation","Transcatheter valve replacement","Start a nitrate for prevention"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. All patients with evidence of aortic stenosis on echocardiogram should have a cardiology consult to evaluate them and set the frequency of follow-up, even when the finding is mild and incidental. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman raised in a region where rheumatic fever is common has a quivering discomfort in the neck, cold skin and right upper quadrant pain. There is a soft opening snap (a sharp sound just after the second heart sound) and a mid-diastolic rumble at the left lower sternal border near the xiphoid that grows louder with inspiration. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Mitral stenosis","Pulmonic regurgitation","Tricuspid stenosis","Tricuspid regurgitation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A rumble is stenosis and diastolic narrows it to the mitral or tricuspid valve; the left lower sternal border near the xiphoid and the inspiratory increase make it right-sided, so tricuspid stenosis. Rheumatic fever is its most common cause worldwide. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 67, 65"},{"q":"An 81-year-old woman with no history of atherosclerotic disease asks whether she should start a statin for prevention. Her low-density lipoprotein is 145 mg/dL. What is the most appropriate approach?","choices":["Calculate the Pooled Cohort risk","Clinical assessment and risk discussion","Automatic high-intensity statin","Refuse any statin due to age"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Above 75 in primary prevention the approach is clinical assessment and a risk discussion rather than an automatic prescription. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has a fasting triglyceride of 780 mg/dL and low-density lipoprotein of 110 mg/dL. Diabetes, kidney disease, alcoholism and hypothyroidism have been excluded. Which management plan is most appropriate?","choices":["Observation with a recheck in a year","Ezetimibe with a high-carbohydrate diet","Very low-fat diet plus a fibrate","Bile acid sequestrant with no diet change"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Above 500 mg/dL the plan is a very low-fat diet (under 15% of calories from fat), weight management, physical activity and a fibrate, which lowers the pancreatitis risk. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man describes his chest pressure while holding a clenched fist over his sternum (Levine sign). Which condition does this gesture most suggest?","choices":["Myocardial ischemia","Pancreatitis","Aortic dissection","Biliary colic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The Levine sign, a clenched fist held over the sternum while describing the discomfort, is the classic gesture of ischemic, anginal chest pain. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 16-year-old boy with homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia has progressive atherosclerosis despite maximal drug therapy and apheresis. Which referral may help by supplying the receptors he lacks?","choices":["Bariatric surgery","Carotid ultrasound only","Kidney transplantation","Liver transplantation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Liver transplantation may prove helpful in the homozygous disorder by providing low-density lipoprotein receptors that his own liver lacks or cannot use. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has a blood pressure of 224/126 with no headache, no visual change, no chest pain, a normal neurological examination and a normal creatinine. How should this be classified?","choices":["A hypertensive emergency, because the systolic exceeds 220","Hypertensive encephalopathy","Severe asymptomatic hypertension","Resistant hypertension"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An emergency is defined by acute hypertension-mediated ORGAN INJURY, not by crossing a numeric threshold. A very high pressure with no acute organ involvement is not an emergency, and that distinction decides how fast the pressure is brought down. It is not a hypertensive emergency. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man is admitted with a large myocardial infarction and develops hypotension, cool extremities and falling urine output from cardiogenic shock. Which infarct feature makes this complication more common?","choices":["A left-sided infarct","Reperfusion inside 90 minutes","A right-sided infarct","A small subendocardial infarct"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cardiogenic shock is more common in left-sided myocardial infarctions, and left ventricular involvement carries a higher mortality. The left ventricle supplies the systemic output that shock takes away. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 78"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman has new swelling of both legs, heavy protein in her urine and low-density lipoprotein of 220 mg/dL. Before a statin is started, which condition should be ruled out?","choices":["Nephrotic syndrome","Familial chylomicronemia","Metabolic syndrome","Heterozygous familial form"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Elevated low-density lipoprotein calls for ruling out nephrotic syndrome, which drives excess very-low-density production and raises triglyceride and low-density lipoprotein. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Which neurohormonal system is a beneficial compensation rather than a driver of damage in heart failure?","choices":["Renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system","Natriuretic peptides","Catecholamine surge","Sympathetic nervous system"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Natriuretic peptides dilate vessels and reduce cardiac load. The sympathetic nervous system and the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system exacerbate the damage and drive remodeling. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A patient's creatinine rises markedly after an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor is started. What does that suggest?","choices":["An expected effect requiring no further thought","Primary aldosteronism","Bilateral renal artery stenosis","Obstructive sleep apnea"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Blocking the renin-angiotensin system removes the efferent arteriolar constriction that was maintaining filtration across a stenosis, so the finding points at the anatomy rather than at drug toxicity. A hemodynamically significant stenosis in a solitary functioning kidney can do the same, because nothing else compensates. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman faints while having blood drawn. Just before, she felt warm, nauseated and sweaty, and she recovered fully within a minute of lying down. Her examination and standing readings afterward are normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Neurogenic orthostatic hypotension","Cardiogenic arrhythmic syncope","Situational reflex syncope","Vasovagal reflex syncope"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fear and pain are vasovagal triggers, a prodrome of warmth, nausea and sweating precedes it, and it is most common in young women; recovery is rapid and complete. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man with reduced ejection fraction heart failure faints abruptly while walking after a brief racing heartbeat. After his electrocardiogram, which further test helps capture an intermittent arrhythmia?","choices":["Cardiac monitoring","A patient log alone","Orthostatic vitals","A tilt-table test"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The arrhythmia work-up is an electrocardiogram, cardiac monitoring and a stress test, together with cardiology consultation. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which setting is typical of micturition syncope?","choices":["Diarrhea for several days","Voiding while standing at night","Shock at hearing bad news","Exertion while climbing stairs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nocturnal urination after abruptly standing from bed, voiding in the standing position, triggers an exaggerated autonomic reflex with vagal activity and reduced vascular tone. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman has recurrent lightheadedness on standing. Her orthostatic readings meet criteria, but her history does not make clear whether the cause is autonomic failure or something else. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Reassure her and stop evaluating","Refer to the right specialty to confirm","Start a vasopressor in clinic","Repeat readings until they normalize"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. History is the most important tool for the source of orthostatic syncope, or for referring to the right specialty for confirmation when it remains unclear. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman with long-standing diabetes and numb feet has a 30 mmHg standing pressure fall with a heart rate rise of only 3 beats and no sign of dehydration. What is the most likely mechanism of her syncope?","choices":["Reduced volume from fluid loss","A vagal surge with vasodilation","Inadequate norepinephrine release","A fast rhythm reducing output"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Autonomic dysfunction leaves too little norepinephrine released from sympathetic postganglionic neurons, so vessels fail to constrict and the heart rate rises less than expected. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which artery supplies the posteromedial papillary muscle?","choices":["Right marginal branch","Diagonal branch","Anterior interventricular branch","Posterior interventricular branch"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The posteromedial papillary muscle is supplied by the posterior interventricular branch alone. The anterolateral papillary muscle has two sources: the anterior interventricular and circumflex branches. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman has severe congenital pulmonic stenosis with exertional dyspnea and syncope. Which procedure is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Aortic valve replacement","Mitral balloon valvotomy","Pulmonic balloon valvuloplasty","Tricuspid annuloplasty"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Severe pulmonic stenosis with symptoms is treated with pulmonic balloon valvuloplasty, with diuretics for right heart failure symptoms; severe congenital disease may require valve replacement. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Which auscultatory finding is typical of mitral valve prolapse?","choices":["A mid-systolic click at the apex","A diastolic rumble near the xiphoid","An opening snap after the second sound","A blowing diastolic murmur to the apex"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mitral valve prolapse gives a mid-systolic click at the apex. It is usually benign and asymptomatic, but it may develop the symptoms and signs of mitral regurgitation. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"How do hyperthyroidism and hypothyroidism differ in their effect on blood pressure?","choices":["Both raise systolic pressure with tachycardia","Neither affects blood pressure","Hyperthyroidism raises systolic; hypothyroidism raises diastolic","Hyperthyroidism raises vascular resistance and hypothyroidism raises output"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the heart rate separates them further: tachycardia accompanies the systolic pattern and bradycardia the diastolic one, so the pulse points to which is operating. Hyperthyroidism does it by raising cardiac output, hypothyroidism by raising vascular resistance. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man with six hours of atypical chest discomfort has a normal electrocardiogram and two high-sensitivity troponin I values of 4 ng/L. Which disposition is most appropriate?","choices":["Discharge with follow-up","Urgent cardiac catheterization","Admit to intensive care","Start thrombolytic therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Troponin is a tool that says who can safely go home. Two high-sensitivity troponin I values of 4 ng/L, far below the men's 20 ng/L, with a normal electrocardiogram rule out an infarct. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with chest pain has a nuclear stress test showing a perfusion defect that does not reverse at rest. What does this fixed defect indicate?","choices":["Prior infarction","Reversible ischemia","Coronary vasospasm","A normal study"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. On a nuclear stress test, a reversible perfusion defect indicates ischemia, while a non-reversible (fixed) defect indicates infarction. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman with rheumatic mitral stenosis has an echocardiogram showing a markedly enlarged left atrium. Which complication is she at greatest risk of developing?","choices":["Concentric hypertrophy","Aortic root dilation","Atrial fibrillation","Left ventricular dilation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In mitral stenosis the left atrium enlarges under raised pressure, and left atrial enlargement predisposes to an abnormal rhythm such as atrial fibrillation, which is common in severe disease. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 35, 39"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man has breathlessness and orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat). His chest radiograph shows blunted costophrenic angles, cephalization (prominent upper-lobe vessels) and an enlarged cardiac silhouette. Which study best measures his ejection fraction?","choices":["A repeat chest radiograph","Right heart catheterization","Multigated acquisition scan","Twelve-lead electrocardiogram"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The radiograph supports heart failure with effusion, cephalization and an increased cardiothoracic ratio, but cannot measure function. The multigated acquisition scan is a very accurate measure of ejection fraction. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man had an ejection fraction of 25% two years ago. On guideline-directed therapy his repeat echocardiogram now shows 46%. How is his heart failure now classified?","choices":["Improved ejection fraction","Preserved ejection fraction","Resolved heart failure","Mildly reduced ejection fraction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A fraction that was 40% or less and has recovered above 40% is improved ejection fraction. The number alone would read mildly reduced, but his history is what defines the group. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with diabetic autonomic neuropathy has recently begun fainting on standing. She has no supine hypertension (high pressure while lying flat), heart disease or kidney disease. Which is the most appropriate initial management?","choices":["Strict fluid restriction each day","Slow position changes, treat the cause","Fludrocortisone before anything else","A vasopressor infusion in clinic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Slow positional changes and treatment of the underlying condition are first-line for orthostatic syncope; drugs come in when these are not enough. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What level of high-density lipoprotein cholesterol is classified as low?","choices":["Below 60 mg/dL","Below 40 mg/dL","Below 100 mg/dL","Below 150 mg/dL"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. High-density lipoprotein below 40 mg/dL is low and counts as a major coronary risk factor, while 60 mg/dL or more is high and removes one risk factor from the count. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 40"}]};
